// Copy to ~/.config/mailfathom/config/10-mailfathom.json and edit — this file stays in the repository and is never the
// one the container reads. The extension matters: MailFathom layers in `*.json` only, so an example or a `.bak` left in
// that directory is ignored. Files are layered in file-name order, so `20-…` overrides `10-…`.
//
// Nothing here is a secret. Every credential is a *reference* to material systemd decrypted for the unit and placed in
// the directory mailfathom.container mounts at /etc/mailfathom/credentials, which is what the container's
// CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY points at. Every name below therefore has to match, in three places that are one decision: the
// `--name=` given to `systemd-creds --user encrypt`, the credential id on the `LoadCredentialEncrypted=` line in
// mailfathom.container, and the reference here. See docs/operations/secret-provisioning.md for the reference grammar
// and docs/operations/deployment-quadlet.md for the commands that produce the files.
//
// This is the one thing that differs from the Compose deployment's example, and it is the reason this shape exists: a
// `file:` reference names material sitting in plaintext on the host, and a `systemd-credential:` reference names
// material that is ciphertext at rest and is decrypted into unswappable memory the moment the unit starts.
//
// The database is configured by mailfathom.container through the environment block, which outranks this file, so it is
// deliberately absent here.
{
  // Uncomment this block when a recorded account authenticates with OAuth rather than with a password, and uncomment
  // the matching LoadCredentialEncrypted= line in mailfathom.container with it. MailFathom seals the refresh token that
  // account's authorization server rotates, and the key it seals under is provisioned like any other credential. The
  // mailbox this file's own comments show uses a password, so a deployment copied from it seals nothing and needs no
  // key at all: an absent section is a valid configuration rather than an omission, and MailFathom starts without one.
  //
  // The material is base64 decoding to exactly 32 bytes — `openssl rand -base64 32`, never the `-base64 33` that
  // generates the database passwords. `KeyId` is written beside every value this key seals, so it is chosen once and
  // never edited afterwards; a date reads well because it says when the key entered service.
  //
  // **Back the key's own base64 up with the database, and never the `.cred` file instead.** `systemd-creds` seals that
  // file against this machine, so it opens nowhere else — see
  // docs/operations/secret-provisioning.md#what-an-encrypted-credential-is-bound-to.
  //
  // "DataEncryption": {
  //   "ActiveKeyId": "2026-08",
  //   "Keys": [
  //     {
  //       "KeyId": "2026-08",
  //       "Material": {
  //         "Name": "mailfathom-data-key",
  //         "SecretReference": "systemd-credential:mailfathom-data-key"
  //       }
  //     }
  //   ]
  // },

  // The synchronization worker's own settings. The mailboxes are not among them: a deployment declares no mail account
  // in its own configuration, because every account belongs to the user whose record holds it. So a deployment copied
  // from this file starts holding nobody's mailbox, reports itself started, and serves the first one recorded against
  // it without a restart:
  //
  //   mfctl user account add --from-file mailbox.json
  //
  // What goes in `mailbox.json` is the object this section used to carry, references and all — the credential stays a
  // reference to material this deployment already holds, exactly as every reference in this file is:
  //
  //   {
  //     "AccountId": "primary",
  //     "DisplayName": "Personal mail",
  //     "Host": "imap.example.test",
  //     "Port": 993,
  //     "UserName": "you@example.test",
  //     "Secrets": {
  //       "Password": {
  //         "Name": "imap-primary-password",
  //         "SecretReference": "systemd-credential:imap-primary-password"
  //       }
  //     },
  //     "TransportSecurity": { "ConnectionSecurity": "TlsOnConnect" },
  //     "Folders": [ { "Alias": "inbox", "SpecialUse": "Inbox" } ]
  //   }
  //
  // `mfctl user add` records a second person, and `--user` then says whose mailbox is being declared. A file still
  // carrying an `Accounts` collection here stops the start, naming those two commands.
  "MailSynchronization": {
    "Enabled": true,
    "Interval": "00:05:00"
  },
  // The administrative endpoint, which is how the mailbox above is recorded: `mfctl user account add` is a client of
  // this surface, and nothing else writes a user's record. It is on for that reason rather than as an extra. Its own
  // default port is 8080, which is the socket the MCP endpoint below is served on, so it states 8090 — the port
  // mailfathom.container publishes for it, on loopback. The key is a systemd credential like every other one here,
  // encrypted under the name `admin-api-key` and loaded by its own LoadCredentialEncrypted= line.
  "AdminEndpoint": {
    "Enabled": true,
    "Port": 8090,
    "Authentication": [
      { "ApiKey": { "Name": "admin", "SecretReference": "systemd-credential:admin-api-key" } }
    ]
  },
  "McpEndpoint": {
    "Enabled": true,
    "Authentication": [
      { "Method": "api-key" }
    ],
    "Cors": {
      "AllowedOrigins": []
    }
  }
}
